<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Banat of Craiova</h1><p class="st-meta">1718 CE – 1739 CE · capital Craiova</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Habsburg-ruled Oltenia, the western third of Wallachia, held by Austria for two decades after the Treaty of Passarowitz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-turkish-war-1716-1718">Austro-Turkish war of 1716 to 1718</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a>, the part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wallachia">Wallachia</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olt-river">Olt</a> river. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> in 1718 ceded it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, which organised it as a province with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>, the traditional residence of the Wallachian bans.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province was governed by an imperial administration with a local boyar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gheorghe-cantacuzino">Gheorghe Cantacuzino</a>, as ban at first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> introduced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, regular taxes, a postal service and stricter control over boyars and monasteries, reforms that curbed noble privileges but raised burdens; many peasants fled across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olt-river">Olt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> religion and Romanian-speaking society remained unchanged.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> defeat in the war of 1737 to 1739 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade">Treaty of Belgrade</a> in 1739, which returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oltenia">Oltenia</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wallachia">Wallachia</a> under Ottoman suzerainty. The episode left detailed Austrian maps and surveys of the region.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banat-of-craiova.jpg" alt="Banat of Craiova" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: ThrashedParanoid and Peregrine981. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Habsburg_Monarchy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1718 CE – 1739 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Craiova</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gheorghe Cantacuzino</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Romania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Habsburg Oltenia, Oltenia (1718-1739)</dd></dl></aside></div>
